# A call the compiler knows will fail when it runs - a builtin given no
# argument at all, or defined() given two - still compiles, and raises its
# error when it runs, where a try can catch it. The compiled program holds
# such a call as a record of the error to raise (the chunk's `throws`
# pool); a stored image (mylang -c) keeps the record, and loading the
# image raises the same error. The image round trip in driver_checks
# compiles this program, so it is also what puts that record through the
# writer, the loader and the -vd dump.

var r = "";
try {
    append();
} catch (InvalidNumberOfArgsEx) {
    r += "a";
}
func f() {
    try {
        defined(runtime(1), 2);
    } catch (InvalidNumberOfArgsEx) {
        return "d";
    }
    return "x";
}
r += f();
assert(r == "ad");
print("ok");
